kReleaseMode is not i am in dev make use fake simualerter:  for turn by turn i foudn this old fake simualt epelase verify a gaint laetts d oc: Yes, there are two ways to do this on an emulator. You can use the HERE SDK's built-in software simulator, or you can route fake hardware signals through the emulator itself.

The software simulator is the fastest path because it eliminates the configuration lag between the OS and the map.

---

### THE PLAIN PICTURE

An emulator’s default GPS state is static. It emits one single coordinate over and over. As we established, static coordinates cause the `VisualNavigator` to stay in tracking mode because it has no velocity or path progression.

To force turn-by-turn navigation to activate, you must feed the navigator a continuous sequence of `Location` objects that possess four non-negotiable properties: changing coordinates, valid timestamps, a heading (bearing), and a speed greater than zero.

---

### THE REAL EXAMPLE

The HERE SDK provides a `LocationSimulator` that automatically generates these exact values along your calculated route geometry. Here is how you swap your live stream out for the simulator inside your `NavStateNotifier` without changing your state machine architecture:

```dart
LocationSimulator? _simulator;

void startDrive(Route route) {
  final mapController = ref.read(mapControllerProvider);
  if (mapController == null) return;

  _teardown();

  final nav = VisualNavigator();
  _navigator = nav;

  nav.startRendering(mapController);
  applyUkUnitsToNavigator(nav);

  nav.routeDeviationListener = _DeviationAdapter(onDeviation);
  nav.destinationReachedListener = _ArrivalAdapter(onArrival);
  nav.routeProgressListener = _ProgressAdapter(onProgress);

  nav.route = route;
  nav.cameraBehavior = DynamicCameraBehavior();

  mapController.gestures.panListener = PanListener(
    (gestureState, origin, translation, velocity) {
      if (gestureState == GestureState.begin) onPan();
    },
  );

  _simulator = LocationSimulator.withRoute(route, LocationSimulatorOptions());
  _simulator!.listener = nav;
  _simulator!.start();

  ref.read(mapControllerProvider.notifier).hideCheckpoints();
  ref.read(driveProgressProvider.notifier).state = null;
  ref.read(mapControllerProvider.notifier).clearRoutePolylineAndOrigin();
  ref.read(sheetStateNotifierProvider.notifier).peek();

  state = ActiveDrive(route: route);
}

```

Make sure you update your `_teardown` method to stop the simulator when the drive ends or resets:

```dart
void _teardown() {
  _recenterTimer?.cancel();
  _recenterTimer = null;

  _locationSub?.cancel();
  _locationSub = null;

  _simulator?.stop();
  _simulator = null;

  ref.read(mapControllerProvider)?.gestures.panListener = null;

  final nav = _navigator;
  if (nav == null) return;
  nav.routeDeviationListener = null;
  nav.destinationReachedListener = null;
  nav.routeProgressListener = null;
  nav.cameraBehavior = null;
  nav.route = null;
  nav.stopRendering();
  _navigator = null;
}

```

---

### BREAK IT

If you want to use the emulator’s native location playback tools (like Android Emulator Extended Controls or Xcode's Location Simulation) instead of the software simulator, your code will break if you use standard location packages blindly.

If you use a basic fake GPS app or emulator route playback tool, the code will compile, the stream will fire, but the turn-by-turn UI will still hang.

---

### FIND THE ROOT

When an emulator plays back a GPX file via its native location controls, it frequently strips out or zeroes out the metadata fields.

If you look at your `_forwardLocation` function, it relies on `pos.heading` and `pos.speed`. Emulators often return `-1.0` or `0.0` for these values during playback routes. When the HERE SDK map-matcher receives a location update where speed is 0 and heading is missing, it assumes the vehicle is stationary and refuses to update the maneuver index.

---

### FIX IT

If you choose to use the emulator's hardware controls rather than the internal `LocationSimulator`, you must explicitly calculate or mock the missing metadata properties right before you pass the object to the engine:

```dart
void _forwardLocation(Position pos) {
  final nav = _navigator;
  if (nav == null) return;
  final loc = Location.withCoordinates(
    GeoCoordinates(pos.latitude, pos.longitude),
  );
  loc.time = pos.timestamp;
  loc.bearingInDegrees = pos.heading >= 0 ? pos.heading : 0.0;
  loc.speedInMetersPerSecond = pos.speed >= 0 ? pos.speed : 13.4; // Forced ~30mph
  loc.horizontalAccuracyInMeters = pos.accuracy;
  nav.onLocationUpdated(loc);
}

```

---

### THE DRILL

1. Drop the `LocationSimulator` block into your `startDrive` method as shown in the example code above.
2. Launch your emulator, pick a destination to compute a valid route, and tap your **Let's Go** CTA.


3. Hit **Start drive**.


4. Watch your debug console. If the `_ProgressAdapter` fires, you will see your remaining distance decrease automatically every second.

### THE LANDING

The software `LocationSimulator` is the cleanest way to test turn-by-turn behavior because it generates flawless mathematical metadata matching the route perfectly. Keep the simulator active for all your UI and logic validation. Save the live `Geolocator` stream configuration exclusively for physical device deployments.

